  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Chorotí\"\nsubtitle: \"Argentina\"\nregion: \"Latin America\"\ntags: [ethnography, latin-america]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Chorotí, also called Chorote, Yofúaha, and Tsoloti |\n| Where | Northwestern Argentina, with communities in Paraguay and southeastern Bolivia |\n| How many | Population not documented |\n| Language | Chorote, a Matacoan language closely related to Wichí |\n| Religion | Not documented in sources |\n| Known for | Chaguar plant-fiber netted bags and clothing · Gourd rattles and whistles for music and ritual · Shell and feather body ornaments · Death dancer's ritual objects |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Chorotí are an indigenous people of northwestern Argentina whose material culture is built from the dry Chaco environment: chaguar plant fiber, gourd, bone, wood, reed, leather, shell and feathers. They speak Chorote, a Matacoan language split into two distinct varieties by modern linguists. Museum collections document their textiles, clothing, musical instruments, and body ornaments, but little else about their practices or beliefs.\n\n## Material culture\n\nChorotí material culture draws from locally available plants, animals and natural materials of the Chaco, combined into functional and ceremonial objects.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nChaguar plant fiber is the textile staple, worked into nets, tunics and cordage alongside wool woven in brown and beige stripes.\n\n- **Chaguar net bags** — Bags worked from chaguar plant fiber, also used to make fishing nets.\n- **Chaguar tunic** — A war shirt made of chaguar fiber used in ceremonial and male dress.\n- **Striped wool cloak** — Two rectangles of wool woven with brown and beige stripes, sewn together lengthwise with hemmed edges.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nChorotí dress combines chaguar fiber, wool, leather, cloth and shell into tunics, cloaks, belts and caps.\n\n- **War shirt** (*chaguar tunic*) — A chaguar fiber tunic with ceremonial or martial function.\n- **Striped wool cloak** — The outer garment, made from two striped wool panels joined lengthwise.\n- **Leather belt with fringe** — A belt accessory made of leather and carrying a fringe.\n- **Shell-and-cloth cap** — A cap combining cloth with shell material.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nPottery water vessels and gourd containers serve everyday needs, while bone and wood tools handle work and household tasks.\n\n- **Pottery water vessels** — Bottle-jars of pottery combined with cord, made to be carried or suspended.\n- **Gourd spoon** — A gourd carved into a spoon for eating.\n- **Gourd water scoop** — A gourd shaped for drawing or carrying water.\n- **Bone awl and tattooing tool** — A bone instrument that served as both a work awl and a body-marking tool.\n- **Fish-jawbone lice comb** — A comb made from the lower jawbone of a fish.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nOrnaments are made from feathers, shell, wood, leaf, skin and imported glass beads, worn on the head, neck, wrists, ankles, ears and fingers.\n\n- **Turkey feather head ornament** — Two turkey feathers joined at the shaft ends with the vanes cut away to leave triangles along the shaft.\n- **Shell disc necklace** — Equal-sized circular shell discs strung on a single fiber strand.\n- **Down and cloth anklets** — A pair of circlets made from down, cloth and vegetable fiber.\n- **Wooden ear-plugs** — Pairs of wooden cylinders, each with a hole carved in one side.\n- **Lizard-skin finger ring** — A ring made from lizard skin.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nChorotí instruments are aerophones and rattles, with whistles of bone, reed and wood and gourd rattles for ritual use.\n\n- **Bone whistle** — A bone whistle with a single hole carved near one end.\n- **Reed whistle** — A reed whistle with a mouthpiece at the top, three holes on the lower half, and the top sealed with resin.\n- **Breast whistle** — A wooden ovoid worn on the breast, incised with triangles, hung from a fiber band beaded with glass and shell.\n- **Death dancer's rattle** — A gourd rattle incised with geometric designs, carried by a dancer in mortuary rites.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nDance is documented only through death dancer's rattles used in mortuary performance.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nThe only documented ritual is a mortuary rite involving a death dancer who carries gourd rattles.\n\n- **Death dancer mortuary rite** — A mortuary performance in which a death dancer carries incised gourd rattles.\n\n## Foodways\n\nChorotí food practices are reflected in equipment for fishing, water transport and fire-making.\n\n- **Plant-fiber fishing net** — A net made from spun chaguar plant-fiber string for catching fish.\n- **Pottery water vessels with cord** — Bottle-jars rigged with cord for transport and storage of water.\n- **Wooden fire-stick** — A stick used by men for making fire.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nNo Chorotí folktales, epics, proverbs or storytelling traditions are documented in available sources.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nChorote is a Matacoan language with two recognized varieties, spoken in northwestern Argentina and across borders into Paraguay and Bolivia; religious practice is not documented.\n\n- **Chorote language** (*Chorote, Yofúaha, Tsoloti*) — A Matacoan language with two distinct varieties, closely related to Wichí and spoken across northwestern Argentina, Paraguay, and southeastern Bolivia.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *chaguar* — A plant fiber used for nets, string, tunics and cordage\n- *Chorote* — The language of the Chorotí people; also Yofúaha and Tsoloti\n- *Yofúaha* — An alternative name for the Chorote language\n- *Tsoloti* — An alternative name for the Chorote language\n- *Matacoan* — A language family to which Chorote belongs\n- *Wichí* — The language most closely related to Chorote within the Matacoan family\n- *chaguar tunic* — A war shirt made of chaguar fiber\n- *death dancer* — A ritual performer in Chorotí mortuary rites who carries gourd rattles\n- *gourd rattle* — A sound-making instrument made from gourd, used in death dancer rituals\n- *breast whistle* — A wooden whistle worn on the chest, decorated with incised triangles and beaded fiber band\n- *bone awl* — A bone tool serving both utilitarian and body-marking functions\n- *lice comb* — A grooming tool made from fish jawbone\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Chorotí language, Wikipedia: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chorot%C3%AD_language\n- Gordon, Raymond G., Jr. (ed.), Ethnologue: Languages of the World, 15th edition, SIL International, 2005\n- The British Museum online collection, which holds the Chorotí objects shown in this atlas (pottery water vessels, chaguar netting and tunic, gourd rattles and whistles, feather and shell ornaments): https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection\n- UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage, Argentina: State page: https://ich.unesco.org/en/state/argentina\n",
